    },
    {
      "heading": "Irregulars and spelling",
      "body": "Five that don't follow the *yo* rule: *ir → vaya / vayan*, *ser → sea / sean*, *saber → sepa*, *estar → esté*, *dar → dé*.\n\nSpelling changes keep the sound: *buscar → busque*, *llegar → llegue*, *empezar → empiece*.\n\nIn Latin America, *ustedes* commands are used for **any** group — friends included: *¡Niños, vengan aquí!* Stem changes carry over too: *seguir → siga*, *doblar → doble*."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Irregular tú commands",
      "body": "You met these in A1; now use them actively: *di* (decir), *haz* (hacer), *ve* (ir), *pon* (poner), *sal* (salir), *sé* (ser), *ten* (tener), *ven* (venir). *¡Haz la tarea! ¡Ven aquí! ¡Pon la mesa! ¡Sé bueno!*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pronouns attach to affirmative commands",
      "body": "*Siéntese* (sit down — usted, from *sentarse*), *Dígame* (tell me — usted, also how many people answer the phone), *Pásenme la sal* (pass me the salt — ustedes), *Hazlo* (do it), *Ponte la chaqueta* (put your jacket on), *Dímelo*. Add an accent when needed to keep the stress: *diga → dígame*."
    }
  ],
